[[Balisca]] is a [[W:Official multilingualism|multilingual state]], where [[W:Multilingualism|multilingualism]] is widely encouraged at the communal and cantonal levels. The most commonly used language in the Baliscan Confederation is [[Baliscano language|Baliscano]], which is itself the world's most widely spoken [[W:Creole language|creole language]]. Baliscano has effectively been the official language of the entire confederation since the [[1885 Baliscan constitutional reforms]]. 

As of the [[2025 Baliscan census|2025 census]], Baliscano was determined to be spoken proficiently by 84% of the Baliscan population, Mercian by 30%, Mozbayani by 16%, and Kurupirangüe by 4%.

The linguistic diversity of Balisca reflects its unique history of widespread immigration and its identity as a "[[Mosaico cultural|cultural mosaic]]" and "[[W:Polyethnicity|polyethnicity]]". Over the course of its history, numerous languages have been brought into the [[Northern Triangle]] from [[Illypnia]] (especially the [[Andalusian Peninsula]], [[Thermessa]], [[Volisania]], [[Breisland]], [[Sarta]] and [[Echia]]), [[Kaftia]] (especially [[Southwestern Kaftia]] and [[Central Kaftia]]), [[Fosia]] (including the [[Bayani world]], the [[Jade world]], [[Kemal]], [[Nusaraya]] and the [[Sanese archipelago]]), and other parts of [[Lurando-Abyala]] (including [[Potocsí]], [[Asikyira]], [[Lacashe]], [[tba]], [[tba]]). Many of these languages have developed into distinct [[W:Dialect|dialects]] and dialect families (e.g., [[Bayaran Aragonese|Bayaran Jacano]]), while others have contributed to the formation of creole languages (like Baliscano itself) and [[W:Pidgin|pidgin]] languages (such as [[Fronteiriço]] and [[Maroniko]]), which serve as linguistic bridges between communities. Beyond Baliscano and the indigenous [[Kurupiran languages]] and [[W:Mapudungun language|Walldungu]], other territorialized languages officially recognized and promoted include [[Xacano language|Xacano/Jacano language]], [[W:Spanish language|Sartan]], [[W:Basque language|Bizkarran]], [[W:Judaeo-Spanish language|Ladino]] (Judaeo-Sartan), [[Volisanian language|Volisanian]], [[Mozbayani]], and [[Beron language|Beron]]. Some of the most spoken foreign languages used by immigrant communities include [[W:Levantine Arabic|Levantine]] and [[Kaftian Bayani]], [[W:Chinese languages|Jade]] ([[W:Varieties of Chinese|all varieties]]), [[Imo language|Imo]], [[W:Tamazight|Tamazight]], [[W:Neapolitan language|Echian]] and [[W:Portuguese language|Kastrunetian]].

Balisca's extensive multilingualism means that a significant portion of its residents are bilingual or multilingual. The nation encourages the widespread use of multiple languages at home, in public spaces, and within the [[Education in Balisca|educational system]]. This policy ensures the preservation of diverse linguistic heritages and facilitates active participation in Balisca's decentralized, direct democracy.

